  body: `
<p>The commute is the single factor that makes or breaks living in Obour. Done right, it is a manageable routine with real public options. Done blind, it is a daily surprise. This guide compares the three real options — LRT, bus and microbus, and driving — on the two things that matter: time reliability and monthly cost.</p>
<p>Editorial note: schedules, fares, and road conditions change by official decision and ongoing works. Verify current timings and fares with the operator before your first commute, and treat all travel times here as estimates, not a timetable.</p>

<h2>Option 1: The LRT electric train</h2>
<p>The <a href="/lrt-obour/">LRT</a> (Arabic guide) changed Obour's commute fundamentally: a fixed-schedule rail link into Greater Cairo's network via the Adly Mansour interchange, immune to road congestion. It wins when your destination sits on or near the line, or connects with a single short hop. The trade-off is the last mile — from the station to your home or office — which you should measure before committing to it as your daily mode.</p>

<h2>Option 2: Buses and microbuses</h2>
<p>The workhorse of the corridor. Public buses and microbuses on the Cairo-Ismailia Desert Road connect Obour with multiple points inside Cairo at popular prices. Coverage and cost are the strengths; peak-hour congestion and variable comfort are the costs. Find your nearest stop through the <a href="/en/transport/">transport guide</a>, then verify the line's reliability in person at your actual commuting hour.</p>

<h2>Option 3: Driving</h2>
<p>Door-to-door freedom via the desert road or the Middle Ring Road for western and New Cairo destinations (see our <a href="/middle-ring-road-obour/">ring road guide</a>). Compute the real monthly figure — fuel, tolls where applicable, wear — before assuming the car is cheap, and compare it honestly against a month of LRT plus occasional ride-hailing.</p>

<h2>Head-to-head comparison</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Mode</th><th>Time reliability</th><th>Monthly cost</th><th>Best for</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td><strong>LRT</strong></td><td>Highest — unaffected by traffic</td><td>Low and predictable</td><td>Destinations on or near the line</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Bus / microbus</strong></td><td>Variable — traffic-dependent</td><td>Lowest</td><td>Budget-first commuters with flexible hours</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Driving</strong></td><td>Variable, worst at peaks</td><td>Highest all-in</td><td>Scattered destinations, off-peak schedules</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>

<h2>Peak-hour strategy</h2>
<ul>
<li>Morning peak flows toward Cairo, evening peak flows back — shifting your departure by even 30 minutes changes the experience.</li>
<li>Never judge the commute by a Friday test. Run a full working week before deciding.</li>
<li>Check <a href="/travel-times/">travel time estimates</a> against your real tracked times and adjust as road works change.</li>
<li>Considering moving to Obour? Do the actual door-to-door round trip twice before signing anything.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Bottom line</h2>
<p>There is no universally best Obour-Cairo commute — there is a best commute for your destination, hours, and budget. Start with the LRT if your destination is on the line; otherwise test buses against driving across one real week. The <a href="/en/transport/">full transport guide</a> covers every mode in detail.</p>
`,
